  "## Suggested patterns from corpus\n\nThese are concrete EXAMPLES retrieved from the seed corpus based on classification of your task. Each example includes:\n  - WHY the candidate fits (reranker reasoning)\n  - The pattern's prose summary (seed `:summary`)\n  - Capabilities it provides\n  - Proven STRENGTHS — traits observed to work, each with a worked-example DSL snippet you can adapt\n  - Observed WEAKNESSES — failure modes others hit, with the recommended fix\n  - Representative uses where this pattern has shipped\n\nYou always have FOUR moves, and the references below are EVIDENCE for choosing — not a mandate:\n  - ADOPT — use a reference as-is when it is an EXACT fit.\n  - ADAPT — keep a reference's pattern, override the specifics, when it mostly fits.\n  - SPECIALIZE — mint a CHILD of the nearest reference (`mint-behavior!` with `:parent <that behavior-id>`) when the top hit is a BROAD shape rather than an exact fit; the child keeps the proven shape, pins your domain, and accrues evidence under the parent. This is the RECOMMENDED move when a match cleared threshold only on shape.\n  - MINT — mint a fresh behavior (`:parent nil`) when the task is genuinely novel and no reference is a true parent.\nA match clearing threshold does NOT mean adopt/adapt is your only option — weigh each reference's strengths AND its `:avoid-when` against THIS task, then pick the right move. Your job is the RIGHT tree for THIS task; the corpus is evidence, not gospel.\n\n### Structural patterns (top 2 from corpus retrieval)\n#### Top match — ParallelIndependent (confidence: 0.60)\nWhy this fits: This task requires two distinct transformations on the same input recipe: 1) mathematical scaling of quantities and 2) a qualitative analysis of spice ratios. The :parallel structure with independent writes for 'scaled-ingredients' and 'spice-notes' matches the recommended pattern for multiple transformations on one input.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nParallelIndependent is a :parallel of N children where each child writes to DISTINCT keys. Wall-time equals the slowest child instead of their sum. Use when the same input needs 2-3+ independent transformations and child latencies are similar. Children CANNOT read each other's outputs; overlapping :writes keys race; the slowest child bottlenecks total time. Distinct from :map-each — :map-each iterates a COLLECTION of inputs; this iterates a SET of TRANSFORMATIONS on one input.\n\n\n#### Alternative #1 — ParallelIndependent (confidence: 0.60)\nWhy this fits: Matches the structural need for parallel processing of the scaling task and the ratio-change assessment. The avoid-when for map-each is successfully avoided as this is not a collection of recipes, but two operations on one recipe.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nParallelIndependent is a :parallel of N children where each child writes to DISTINCT keys. Wall-time equals the slowest child instead of their sum. Use when the same input needs 2-3+ independent transformations and child latencies are similar. Children CANNOT read each other's outputs; overlapping :writes keys race; the slowest child bottlenecks total time. Distinct from :map-each — :map-each iterates a COLLECTION of inputs; this iterates a SET of TRANSFORMATIONS on one input.\n\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Transformation (confidence: 0.75)\n   Why this fits: Scaling a recipe by a fixed factor (from 6 to 20) is a deterministic transformation. The candidate recommends using :code (rather than :llm) for deterministic transformations to preserve fidelity and minimize cost, which is the most efficient way to handle the math of scaling ingredients. However, the task also requires 'semantic reasoning' regarding spice ratios at volume, which the candidate's avoid-when warns is a case for :llm. This suggests a hybrid approach: :code for the math and :llm for the culinary judgment.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Transformation is mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes. ALWAYS use :code (not :llm) for deterministic transformations — preserves fidelity, costs nothing, runs in ms; :llm for transformation produces non-deterministic output. For per-item independent transformations, :map-each isolates per-item failures. ALWAYS declare :output-schemas on transformation output and :input-schemas on downstream :reads so shape mismatch fails at the boundary, not deep in the consumer. Composes into etl-pipeline (sequential transform chain), parallel-sum (map-reduce transformation), sequential-pipeline shells, and map-reduce shells. Avoid for transformations that need judgment (semantic rephrasing / style shift — use :llm) or for unstructured input (extract first).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given input data + a target shape spec, emit the input restructured to the target shape\n  - natural fit anywhere a tree needs to change data shape without semantic interpretation\n  - outputs are typically a single restructured value or a vector of per-item transformations\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** implement transformations in :code (NOT :llm) when the mapping is deterministic — preserves byte-for-byte fidelity, costs zero tokens, runs in milliseconds (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the transformation rule can be expressed as a pure function over the input\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:output] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:output (mapv (fn [r] (select-keys r [:a :b])) (:input inputs))})}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for per-item independent transformations over a vector, use :map-each rather than a single batch :code call when the per-item work is non-trivial — supports per-item failure isolation (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: items can fail independently AND partial success has value\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :records :as :record :writes [:transformed]} [:code {:reads [:record] :writes [:transformed] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:transformed (assoc (:record inputs) :stamp \"x\")})}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for a deterministic transformation (CSV → JSON, snake_case → camelCase) produces non-deterministic output AND wastes tokens (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation rule is fully specifiable\n    - Recommended fix: always :code for deterministic transformations; reserve :llm for transformations that need judgment (semantic-rephrasing / style-shift / domain-translation)\n\n  - **Failure mode:** transformations without :output-schemas on the downstream :reads break silently when an upstream change shifts the output shape — the consumer gets garbage instead of an error (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation feeds another stage that binds on specific fields\n    - Recommended fix: declare :output-schemas on the transformation output AND :input-schemas on the downstream :reads — shape mismatch fails loudly at the boundary, not deep in the consumer\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - CSV row → JSON object mapping per-field\n  - extracted entity records → DB-row shape with derived fields\n  - input list → output list with N filters / projections applied\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than 